.he "ld (1)                       9/21/26                       ld (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
ld
- the micronix Z80 link editor
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
ld [ -vV9rs ] [ -o file ] [ -Ldir ] [ -llib ] [ -Ttext=addr ] [ -Tdata=addr ] [ -Tbss=addr ] [ -Sdata|-Stext ] [ -Shigh ] file ...
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Ld links Whitesmiths object files and archives into one executable, or,
with -r, into a relocatable object for a later link.  It reads the
relocations asz wrote, lays out the text, data and bss segments, and
patches symbol references to their final addresses.  The output is a
program and is created executable.
.sp
An archive is read for the members it needs and not linked whole: a
library can hold many objects, and a program pulls in only those that
resolve an undefined symbol.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-v	Verbose; say it again for more detail.

.ti -10
-V	List each object file as it is linked.

.ti -10
-9	Write nine-character symbol names in the output instead of fifteen, for an object that must match one made that way.

.ti -10
-r	Write a relocatable object rather than an executable, keeping the relocations for a later link.

.ti -10
-s	Strip the symbol table from the output.

.ti -10
-o file	Name the output file; the default is a.out.

.ti -10
-Ldir	Search dir for libraries named with -l.

.ti -10
-llib	Link liblib.a, resolving undefined symbols out of it.

.ti -10
-Ttext=addr, -Tdata=addr, -Tbss=addr	Set the origin of a segment.  Text and data default to the origins in the first object; bss follows data.

.ti -10
-Sdata	Fold every section of the input objects - text, data and bss - into the data segment, instead of the usual three-segment layout.  The bss is written out as zero bytes rather than left as a segment, because nothing will clear it.  With -r this is how a piece of code is made into a data-only object that can be parked at a high address.

.ti -10
-Stext	The same fold, but into the text segment.

.ti -10
-Shigh	In a final link, park an unplaced data-only object - a -r -Sdata fold - after bss, at _ebss, rather than in the data segment.  Only objects of that form are moved; ordinary data-only objects still pack normally.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
An undefined symbol that no object or library defines is reported and
the link fails.  The symbol tables of the objects are merged into the
output, one table, so nm reads a linked program the same way it reads an
object.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
CALLING CONVENTIONS
.in +5
.br
A function passes its first argument in A when its prototype says
that parameter is an 8-bit scalar, and returns an 8-bit result in A.
Every other argument and result travels in HL - a long's high half in
HL'.  Each object symbol carries the callee's two bits, written by
asz from a
.bd
.abi name,bits
directive: bit 1, first parameter in A; bit 2, result in A.

The compiler marks every direct call with the convention it used by
choosing one of the reserved conditional-call opcodes:
.bd
call po
(0xE4, arg8/ret8),
.bd
call pe
(0xEC, arg8/ret16), or
.bd
call p
(0xF4, arg16/ret8).  An ordinary
.bd
call
(0xCD) is arg16/ret16.  Ld compares the marker with the callee's two
bits.  When they agree it rewrites the instruction to a plain
.bd
call;
when they do not, the link fails with
.bd
call ABI mismatch,
naming the function and both conventions.  An ordinary call is
checked the same way, so an unprototyped call to a function whose
first parameter is a byte is caught rather than silently given the
word convention.  Only
.bd
po, pe
and
.bd
p
are reserved; the
.bd
nz, z, nc, c
and
.bd
m
conditions are available to hand-written code as before.

With -r the check is not run and a symbol marker is left in the
output, because its callee may not be in this group.  The callee's two
bits are carried through to the output symbol table, so the final link
can still check the call.

A call to a local function is a segment relocation, not a symbol one:
the assembler resolved the address and the object carries no symbol
entry for the callee, so there is nothing to compare.  Ld swizzles
that marker - the opcode alone - to a plain
.bd
call
in every link, -r included.  It has to, and in -r especially: 0xEC is
CALL PE, so a marker left in a folded kernel page would run the call
only when the parity flag happened to say so, and the final link,
finding the page in a data segment, would never look at the opcode.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
ar (1), asz (1), ccc (1), nm (1), size (1)
